A||||6||theogre||Cliff, What is Holland??????
Z||000000||theogre||09-05-99||12:18 PM||||I know this isn't a political forum, but I'm confused. If any one else has followed the world news the last few months I'm not the only one. So I thought I'd ask this here. Hope I'm not getting to personal for anyones comfort.<p>I'm probably not the only one here that always thought Holland was it's own country. To many movies about guys skating the cannals on wood skates, stories about fingers in Dikes, I guess. Am I correct now in thinking its a state/province of a country called the Netherlands? What other "states" do you all have? It's kind of hard to make sense of the rather poor world map I have.<p>I saw a CNN story recently that your government was up for some major changes. Has that affected the daily lives of people in any way, or is it like our congress talking about reducing taxes which is likely to happen when hell freezes for the third time........
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||09-05-99||01:51 PM||||We use both names for one and the same country. "Holland" is a somewhat older name (and actually no longer correct) but is used as often as "The Netherlands". They both translate to "the low country" because the larger part of our country is below sea level (in French, Holland is called "Pays Bas" which also means "low country"). The only thing protecting us from being swallowed by the sea is our dunes and dikes. We've had some large disasters in the past where our dunes couldn't protect us against high tides. The most recent was in 1953 where over 1800 people were killed. This led to the building of the world-famous "Waterworks", protecting the province that was almost completely engulved during this last disaster.<p>Like I briefly mentioned, "Holland" is actually no longer the correct name for our country. It was the name of a country that technically speaking no longer exists...<p>We are referred to as the "Dutch" which is often wrongly translated as "German" (which is "Deutsch"). It acually comes from the dutch word "Doets" (almost pronounced exactly the same as "Dutch") - which was how our ancestors were called. Even our national anthom translates it wrong. One line goes "zijn wij van duitschen bloed" which means "we come from German heritage" while it should be "zijn wij van doetschen bloed" ("we come from Doetschen heritage").<p>Anyway, about the change in government - I have no idea what you (or CNN) is talking about, unless they are talking about the European Union. This means a large part of Europe will become one economic union. We already have two currencies: the dutch guilder and the "Euro" although the latter is only used on paper. Not until 2002 will the guilder (and most other european currencies) disappear and physically be replaced by the Euro. The impact on daily-life? All prices are listed in two different currencies...<p>We already have european law so not much changes there.<p>Hey - you asked!  <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><br>
Z||000002||Cooter||09-05-99||01:59 PM||||Cliff- are you a politician or a school teacher?? Most "normal" people cant spout that much info so fast! Very interesting. . .
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||09-05-99||02:27 PM||||No, I'm not a teacher - but I have this nasty habbit of remembering everything I see or hear...
Z||000004||theogre||09-05-99||04:47 PM||||maybe it wasn't you guys.... I had read, I thought, that your equivalent of parlement/congress was being reorganized to include/exclude one or more partys. I can't find the CNN item right now. Sorry.<p>As for the Euro, I was wondering how that was effecting people. That has to be a real pain in the ass at times. Even tho I don't use it, It's caused updates to nearly every progam I can think of here. Which has definatly been a boil for U.S. companies dealling abroad.
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||09-05-99||05:23 PM||||Perhaps you got confused with Indonesia - a former Dutch colony. It's a mess there right now.<p>About the Euro, the timing couldn't be worse for introducing the Euro... You know, with Y2k just around the corner. If things go wrong, Europe can fall in a deep economical crisis.<p>As for getting used to new currency, the Euro's value is very close to the (US) dollar. Makes calculating exchange rates when I'm on vacation there a whole lot easier!<br>
Z||000006||theogre||09-05-99||08:24 PM||||hope its closer than the Canadian rate. <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15> just teasing you guys in the great white north.
